        "json": {
          "output": {
            "post_title": "The 'Synthetic Green' Trap: AI-Generated Tests Mask Release Risk",
            "post_body": "AI test generators can inflate velocity and test counts by 300%. Teams are celebrating volume and green dashboards while meaningful fault-detection remains unmeasured.\n\nGenerated tests often exercise simple paths, replicate assertions, and add noise without finding defects. Mutation testing and requirement-to-risk traceability are rarely applied to AI-produced suites.\n\nPMO dashboards that display pass rates and test counts become misleading when they lack measures of test effectiveness. Approval gates tied to those dashboards create the illusion of safe releases.\n\nFixing this requires changing what PMOs and QA track. Make mutation score a gating metric. Require traceability from critical risks to tests. Add representative fault seeding and periodic exploratory audits to validate effectiveness.\n\nStart reporting defect escape metrics and test effectiveness ratios alongside velocity. That restores signal to delivery decisions and prevents hollow green approvals.\n\nTakeaway: Do not accept AI-generated test volume as proof of readiness; insist on mutation testing and risk traceability before approving releases.",
            "hash_tags": "#QA #PMO #AIinTesting #MutationTesting #ReleaseRisk #EngineeringLeadership",
            "image_prompt": "A clean technical illustration of a PMO dashboard showing overly green status indicators in the foreground with faint overlays of broken test case icons and mutation symbols behind, using a minimal, professional color palette."
          }
        },
        "pairedItem": {
          "item": 0
        }
